A||||12||Greg Piet||Blue Smoke||||1||
Z||000000||Greg Piet||04-06-2000||07:06 PM||||Ok...  My little v6 is not passing smog!   The reason is not that some value is too high, but rather when they do the High RPM test, then let off on the gas, a puff of smoke (seems blue)comes from the exhaust.  I know rings may be the culprit, but could it also be valve stem seals?  Is there something else im forgetting.<P>I also thought it was white for a while from unburnt fuel(at least it smelled like it), so I checked the cap and its center post was fried (I guess the MSD killed it).  So im back to stock, and now the smoke seems blue.  I also noticed some smoke coming out from the dipstick port.  So im thinking the oil was contaminated by unburnt fuel due to the hosed cap.  uhhhh   am I definately screwed?<BR>Aw heck i dont know right now except that im a grump. ||||reg||1
Z||000001||daved||04-06-2000||09:03 PM||||Only 2 things I can think of that can blow smoke out your dipstick (not being rude!)<BR>Either your PCV valve is not working...stuck or plugged, or its the rings...lots of blow-by.  D.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Ed||04-06-2000||09:12 PM||||PCV valves can be easily checked: Does it rattle? Rattling means the innards are free and that is good.  Better yet, replace it anyway if you haven't for a while, it is a routine maintenance part.  <P>As for the rings, the time honored method of checking them is with a compresssion gauge.  Check for consistency and absolute value.  Squirt a little oil in the cylinder if its low, and if it comes up the ring(s) is bad. <P>Bad valve stem seals usually allow some blue smoke to come out on start-up (only). Mine are slightly bad but its a big deal (expensive at a dealer) to replace them.  <p>[This message has been edited by Ed (edited 04-06-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||fieroshop||04-06-2000||09:41 PM||||Greg, both Daved and Ed are right. It does sound like you have blowby, but check your pvc valve and see if that improves if not you may have to do an overhaul.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Greg Piet||04-06-2000||10:16 PM||||Thats what I was afraid of!!<BR>I just had to check to make sure I wasnt forgettting something simple and silly<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Patrick||04-07-2000||02:25 AM||||<BR>Try using some heavier oil before you tear into the engine. Nothing beats an overhaul, but you'd probably like to pass the smog test for now, right? <P>Drain the oil, change the filter, put in  20W-50 oil, add some STP when she's warmed up, then take her through the test. You might be surprised. Just make sure you allow for the volume of the STP. Overfilling the crankcase is the last thing you want to with an oil burner (or for any engine actually).<P>My Fiero is due to be smog tested within the week and I'll be following my own advice. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Good luck!||||reg||1
Z||000006||Formula||04-07-2000||09:48 AM||||fill it with better gas and octane booster and run it for a couple hours before you take it in, I heard that this works sometimes.||||reg||1
Z||000007||lennyb4||04-07-2000||11:35 AM||||Hate to say it but your probally looking at bad rings. Check your dip stick to see if you have anti-freeze or excessive gas in the oil. In the mean time heres a couple of hints on passing aims. 1st fill up with high octane gas and yes add additive to it (unless you can get 103+ octane around your area, some shell stations carry this) and drive the car around for about an hour. 2nd losen up you PCV valve alittle. And if worst comes to worse replace your plugs the day of the exam. These should help you pass aims (trust me from experiance).||||reg||11
Z||000008||Greg Piet||04-07-2000||03:27 PM||||Well I reluctantly (I am afraid of the truth)did a compression test on 3 cylinders (can you guess which ones???)   One was at 80 while the other 2 were at 110.   So yes it seems like the rings need to be done.  <P>The GM book implies that Rings can be done w/o yanking the motor, just by removing the heads and the oil pan I can get the connecting rods off the crank, then I can pull pistons  out from the top.  The Chilton/Haynes book says that I have to remove the motor...  I CANNOT remove the motor with my present living/garage conditions, so I am going to try it with the motor in place.  Any comments, or suggestions (especially about when I hone the cylinder walls, and keeping the rest of the motor clean)?  <BR>I tried the heavier oil bit (I just used 50W racing oil), and there is still that tiny bit o' smoke coming out from the tailpipes, so ive finally accepted it and im going to tear apart my Fiero.  I have about 2 weeks till I have to pass inspection, so I figure I can get it done by then.<P>Thanks all for your opinions!||||reg||1
Z||000009||Patrick||04-07-2000||03:39 PM||||<BR>Isn't there a chance that the problem is the <I>valves</I> and NOT the rings? <P>Bad valves can obviously lower compression, and worn valve guides, stems, and/or seals can produce the oil burning your engine is exhibiting. Hey, it's just a thought.||||reg||5
Z||000010||Ed||04-07-2000||09:38 PM||||The oil test is a pretty reliable shade tree mechanic test.  The oil squirted into the cylinder temporarily seals up the rings, and the pressure goes up.  Bad valves are not sealed by this test and the pressure won't go up.  <P>Of course, the bad news is, both may be bad.     <BR>Did you squirt oil into the cylinders?   ||||reg||1
Z||000011||Greg Piet||04-08-2000||05:20 AM||||no I didnt do the oil squirt test...   Instead I just yanked the heads off today.  Apparently some dust crept into the intake from the last rallycross...   So Im going to replace the rings as the walls  do not look damaged (But ill still hone the cylinder walls).  There does seem to be some oil creeping up from the pistons so that is the likely culprit.  If the oil is still burning then I guess I will rebuild and then throw in my spare set of heads.||||reg||1
Z||000012||batboy||04-08-2000||07:59 AM||||V6 valve guides and seals are high wear items. I'd have those heads rebuilt if I were you. I don't know how you're going to clean up all the sludge and honing shavings afterwards with the engine block in the car. What about rod and main bearings? You should install new ones. You're really looking at that engine needing a complete rebuild, which means it really should get dropped out of there and done right. Good luck!<p>[This message has been edited by batboy (edited 04-08-2000).]||||reg||1
